## Modelling "is-a"

Composition says one thing *has* another. **Inheritance** says one thing *is a kind of* another. A derived class contains everything its base class has, and can be used wherever the base is expected.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    Animal <|-- Dog
    Animal <|-- Cat
    Animal <|-- Bird
    Bird <|-- Penguin
    class Animal {
        +std::string name
        +int age
        +eat()
    }
    class Dog {
        +fetch()
    }
    class Cat {
        +purr()
    }
    class Bird {
        +double wingspan
    }
```

| Term | Also called | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| **Base class** | Parent, superclass | The class being inherited from |
| **Derived class** | Child, subclass | The class that inherits |
| **Hierarchy** | | The tree formed by the classes |

## Syntax

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Animal
{
public:
    std::string name{};
    int age{};

    void eat() const { std::cout << name << " eats\n"; }
};

class Dog : public Animal
{
public:
    void fetch() const { std::cout << name << " fetches\n"; }
};

int main()
{
    Dog rex{};
    rex.name = "Rex";
    rex.eat();      // inherited from Animal
    rex.fetch();    // Dog's own
    return 0;
}
```

`: public Animal` makes `Dog` a derived class of `Animal`. A `Dog` object physically contains an `Animal`, called its **base subobject**, followed by whatever `Dog` adds.

```
 a Dog object
 ┌─────────────────────────────┐
 │ ┌── Animal subobject ─────┐ │
 │ │ name          age       │ │     the base part comes first
 │ └─────────────────────────┘ │
 │   Dog's own members         │
 └─────────────────────────────┘
```

## Construction and destruction order

A derived object is built from the inside out.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

class Animal
{
public:
    explicit Animal(std::string_view name) : m_name{ name } { std::cout << "Animal constructed\n"; }
    ~Animal() { std::cout << "Animal destroyed\n"; }

protected:
    std::string m_name{};
};

class Dog : public Animal
{
public:
    Dog(std::string_view name, std::string_view breed)
        : Animal{ name }, m_breed{ breed }
    {
        std::cout << "Dog constructed\n";
    }

    ~Dog() { std::cout << "Dog destroyed\n"; }

private:
    std::string m_breed{};
};

int main()
{
    Dog d{ "Rex", "Collie" };
    return 0;
}
```
```
Animal constructed
Dog constructed
Dog destroyed
Animal destroyed
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph Build["Construction"]
        direction LR
        A1["Animal"] --> D1["Dog"] --> P1["Puppy"]
    end
    subgraph Destroy["Destruction"]
        direction LR
        P2["Puppy"] --> D2["Dog"] --> A2["Animal"]
    end
```

| Step | Order |
|---|---|
| Construction | Base class, then the derived class's members, then the derived constructor's body |
| Destruction | Exactly the reverse |

The reasoning is the one from [[6.03 Destructors and Object Lifetime]]: the derived part may depend on the base part, so the base must exist first and outlast it.

> [!note] A derived class initializes its base through a base constructor
> `Dog` cannot write `m_name{ name }` in its own initializer list, because `m_name` is not `Dog`'s member to initialize. It names a base constructor instead: `Animal{ name }`. If none is named, the base's default constructor is used, and if there is none the code does not compile.

## Inheriting constructors

Constructors are not inherited by default. When a derived class adds no data that needs initializing, it can adopt the base's constructors wholesale:

```cpp
class Dog : public Animal
{
public:
    using Animal::Animal;
};
```

## The is-a test

Inheritance is a strong commitment. Before using it, ask whether **every** derived object can stand in for a base object **everywhere** a base is used. This is the idea of *substitutability*.

| Proposed | Verdict | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Circle` is a `Shape` | Yes | Anything valid for a shape is valid for a circle |
| `Stack` is a `Vector` | No | A vector allows insertion anywhere; a stack must not. Use composition. |
| `Square` is a `Rectangle` | Usually no | Code that sets a rectangle's width and height independently breaks a square |

When the test fails, the relationship is "is implemented using", and that is composition: [[7.07 Object Relationships]].

## Check yourself
```quiz
? Which constructor runs first, the base's or the derived class's?
+ The base's; destructors then run in the reverse order, derived first
- The derived class's; the base's runs at the end of it
- Whichever is declared first in the file
- They run at the same time
> The base part must exist before the derived part is built on top of it, and it is torn down last.

? How does a derived constructor initialize base members?
- By assigning to them in the constructor body
+ By naming a base constructor in its member initializer list
- By calling `super()` as the first statement
- It cannot; base members are always default-initialized
> Write `Derived(int x) : Base{ x } { }`. Private base members can only be set through a base constructor.

? What is a base subobject?
- A pointer from the derived object to a separate base object
- A copy of the base class made when the derived class is copied
+ The part of a derived object that is a complete base object embedded in it
- A static member shared by all derived classes
> Every derived object contains a full base object inside it, which is why it can be used wherever a base is expected.

? Why is "a stack is a vector" a poor use of inheritance?
- Because a vector cannot be inherited from
- Because a stack has no members
- Because inheritance makes the stack slower
+ A stack must not allow all that a vector allows
> Public inheritance would expose `insert`, `operator[]` and the rest, breaking the stack's rules. Hold a vector as a member instead.
```
